1912, with a brief restoration in 1917) and later as the Kangde Emperor of Manchukuo, a Japanese puppet state during World War II. After the war, he was held as a war criminal in the Soviet Union and China until 1959, when the Chinese Communist Party granted him amnesty and presented him as a model of successful reeducation.\nIn 1908, chosen by Empress Dowager Cixi to succeed the childless Guangxu Emperor, Puyi ascended the throne at age two with his father Prince Chun as regent. Following the Xinhai Revolution, Empress Dowager Longyu signed the abdication edict on Puyi's behalf; in return, the Articles of Favorable Treatment allowed him to retain his imperial title and remain in the Forbidden City. From 1 to 12 July 1917, the loyalist general Zhang Xun restored him to the throne.","image":"溥仪.jpg","birth":1906.1013698630136,"death":1967.7917808219179}},"media":{"Flag of Manchukuo.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Flag_of_Manchukuo.svg/330px-Flag_of_Manchukuo.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Manchukuo.svg","author":"Urmas","license":"Public domain"},"Emblem of the Emperor of Manchukuo.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Emblem_of_the_Emperor_of_Manchukuo.svg/330px-Emblem_of_the_Emperor_of_Manchukuo.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_the_Emperor_of_Manchukuo.svg","author":"Shuntian","license":"Public domain"},"溥仪.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/%E6%BA%A5%E4%BB%AA.jpg/330px-%E6%BA%A5%E4%BB%AA.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%BA%A5%E4%BB%AA.jpg","author":"Unknown authorUnknown author","license":"Public domain"}},"names":["Büyük Mançurya İmparatorl